Figure 1 returns a value (`return done`), so `drop_disc` becomes a **function**; Figure 2 returns nothing, so `play_game` becomes a **procedure** (`void` in C). Both are placed before `main` (and, in Pascal, before the main block), after the declaration of `c4grid`, and `empty_grid` and `is_aligned` come from the file supplied by the Board.

**Conversion rules.** `←` becomes `=` (C) or `:=` (Pascal); `=` in a condition becomes `==` (C) or stays `=` (Pascal); `≠` becomes `!=` or `<>`; `AND`/`OR` become `&&`/`||` or `and`/`or`; `Print` becomes `printf` or `writeln`; `While ... EndWhile` becomes `while`; `Repeat ... Until(c)` becomes `do { ... } while (!(c))` in C or `repeat ... until c` in Pascal. Because C arrays start at index 0, the pseudocode cell `A[i][col]` (rows and columns numbered from 1) is `A[i - 1][col - 1]` in C.

**drop_disc in ANSI C**

```c
int drop_disc(TBoard A, int col, char disc)
{
    int done = 0, i = ROWS;

    if (col >= 1 && col <= COLS) {
        while (done != 1 && i > 0) {
            if (A[i - 1][col - 1] == ' ') {   /* C arrays start at 0 */
                A[i - 1][col - 1] = disc;
                done = 1;
            }
            i = i - 1;
        }
    } else {
        done = -1;
    }
    return done;
}
```

Trace of `drop_disc` (checked by the Python translation): in an empty grid `drop_disc(c4grid, 3, 'X')` finds row 5 empty and returns 1; calling it again with `'O'` puts the disc in row 4; when column 3 holds 5 discs the next call returns 0 (column full, "Chosen column is full!"); `drop_disc(c4grid, 6, 'X')` and `drop_disc(c4grid, 0, 'X')` return -1 (invalid column).

